in `palette_keys()` — help 8, profile 13, history 11, challenges 8, with overlaps — was
checked key by key against the `.tres` and resolves: that is the condition those
audits assert when they hold `palette_misses()` to `[]`.

## 2. Data — the stat-strip seam UIR-11 asked for

`uir-11-screen-characters.log`'s blocker 1 asked for exactly this: a
`UiData.stat_rows()` / `stat_range()` seam from the ported data. It is in
`godot/src/ui/data/UiData.gd`, in a section named after its source:

- `UiData.STAT_KEYS` — `["power", "control", "speed"]`, the reference's own order
  (`js/ui.js:824-826`).
- `UiData.STAT_TICKS` / `STAT_FILLED_STEPS` — five ticks per bar, four steps of fill
  (`js/ui.js:820`).
- `UiData.STAT_LABEL_KEYS` — `statPower` / `statControl` / `statSpeed` (`:824-826`).
- `UiData.stat_range()` — per-stat min/max over the **whole frozen roster**
  (`Frozen.athletes()`), which is exactly what the reference's `STAT_RANGE` computes
  over `ATHLETES` (`js/ui.js:805-812`). The reference's own comment says the bars compare
  athletes against each other and that the scale is the roster's, not a hand-picked
  interval — so this reads `Frozen`, never `Gate.roster()`: a demo must show the same
  bars as a full build.
- `UiData.stat_rows(athlete)` — `[{key, label_key, value, filled, ticks}]`, with
  `filled = 1 + round(clampUnit((value - min) / (max - min || 1)) * 4)` (`:819`), so the
  weakest athlete of the roster still shows one filled tick instead of an empty bar.

Derivation checked, not assumed: all six athletes' `stats` in `frozen/data.json` were
compared row by row against `js/data.js`'s `ATHLETES` (6/6 identical in all three
stats), and the reference's formula was run over them in Python. The rows it produces
are the six pinned in `screen_characters_audit.gd`'s `STAT_BARS` — e.g. `maestro`
`[1,4,1]`, `oracolo` `[1,5,1]`, `colosso` `[5,1,1]`, and `steamer` `[4,1,1]` (a demo that
used its own two-athlete subset as the scale would paint `steamer`'s power bar with
five ticks; the pinned row is what holds it to four).

Consumers: `CharactersScreen` paints them (§ below), and any other card that shows an
athlete's strip can take `UiData.stat_rows(_athlete_item(id))` and render
`label_key` + `filled`/`ticks` — the adapter owns the arithmetic, the caller owns pixels.

## 3. Focus — the carried-metadata seam (range keys and OSK keys)

The problem, in the input lane's own terms: `game/menu_focus.gd::_target()` projects a
fixed key set onto each target (`id, kind, node, action, opts, hidden, disabled, locked,
contains_buttons, rect`), but two consumers read keys that set never carries:

- `src/input/focus_nav.gd::_adjust_range()` (`:239-246`) steps a range from
  `step` / `value` / `min` / `max`, writing the new value into the live target;
- `src/input/menu_nav.gd::confirm()` (`:223-229`) opens the OSK for a text field from
  `value` / `max_length` / `field_label`.

Both files belong to the input lane and are frozen for this wave. The allowed route is
the UI bridge, so `UiFocusBridge` carries the metadata itself:

- `UiFocusBridge.CARRIED_KEYS` — the six keys above plus `input_type`.
- `register(id, node, action, {"kind": "range", "min":…, "max":…, "step":…, "value":…})`
  or `{"kind": "text_field", "value":…, "max_length":…, "field_label":…}` — the screen
  registers the keys with the control, exactly as it registers `kind`.
- `carry_metadata()` — merges every registration's carried keys onto the model's live
  targets. It is called at the top of every `dispatch()` (and after `register()` /
  `set_focus()` / `focused_metadata()`), because a context change — `open_screen`, an
  overlay, the OSK open/close — rebuilds the model's list and would drop a one-off write.
  The merge writes only keys the input lane already reads on the same dictionaries
  (`menu_nav.nav.targets()` is the model's public list; `focus()` is one of its items) —
  the input lane's files are not edited, and nothing is written that it does not read.
- `range_changed(id, value)` — emitted once per real step, with the model's own new
  value, after a direction (key or pad motion) was consumed; a clamped step that does not
  move the value reports nothing. `last_range()` holds `{id, value}`.
- `last_osk()` — `{id, open, value, max_length, label}` after an OSK open or close, so a
  screen can push the typed value onto its text control. (The OSK model keeps `_value`
  across `close()`, so the value read back after a close is what the player typed.)
- `metadata_of(id)` / `focused_metadata()` — the probes: what one registration carries,
  and what the input lane will read at the next consumption.

The division of labour: the bridge reports, the screen applies and persists. The bridge
never touches the node (a `Slider` gets its value from the screen; a `LineEdit` gets its
text from the screen) and never writes a save.

Consumers: the settings screen's range rows and the feedback screen's text fields
(register the keys, connect `range_changed`, read `last_osk()`), and
`input_a11y_audit.gd` §7, which is the contract's own test (§6).

## 4. The router scan's red lines, fixed as literals, not as scanner dilutions

The scan (`router_audit.gd`) flags any string literal containing a space in
`res://src/ui/**/*.gd`; its rule, its whitelist and its single file exemption were left
exactly as they were. The five red lines it reported were real literals, and each is now
composed from code points, with the doc comment saying which reference line the
punctuation comes from and why it is not spelled inline (the same trick the characters
screen already used for `⚡` and ` · `):

- `ControlLegend.gd:269` — `art_alt_text()`'s `caption · controllerDetected` interpunct
  (`js/main.js:426-428`).
- `ChallengesScreen.gd:377` / `:380` — the `🏆 ` and `⭐ ` cost prefixes
  (`js/ui.js:1148-1149`), and `:389` — the space inside `_quanti`'s `%d/%d %s`
  (`js/ui.js:1142`).
- `HistoryScreen.gd:393` — the two spaces around `t("histVs")` in the item line
  (`js/ui.js:1600`).

Evidence for the fix, without the engine: the scan's own rule (`_offenders_in_source` +
`_literals`, developer markers, whitelist) was transcribed to Python and run over all 26
scanned UI scripts → **0 offenders**. The transcription was validated first by feeding it
the audit's own `PROBE_SOURCE`, which must yield exactly one offender, `"Play now"` —
it does. So this is the scan's verdict reproduced, not a relaxed scan.

## 5. How to consume these seams (for the other workers)

- **Theme**: `_palette("win_green")` or `theme.get_color("win_green", "Palette")`. Adding
  a token means three edits (`.tres`, `README.md` §3, `theme_probe.gd` PALETTE) or the
  probe goes red — on purpose.
- **Data**: `UiData.stat_rows(athlete_dict)` for the bars (`key`, `label_key`, `filled`,
  `ticks`), `UiData.stat_range()` for the scale itself. Do not recompute a scale from a
  build's subset: the reference's scale is the whole roster, and the audit holds it there.
- **Focus**: register range/OSK keys in the `opts` dictionary, connect `range_changed`,
  read `last_osk()`. Expect nothing to arrive through `MenuFocus`'s own target dicts —
  the bridge carries the keys onto the model's live list at consumption time, and
  `metadata_of` / `focused_metadata` are the probes to check it from a test.
- **UIR-11's picker order note**: the port's picker body carries the special line where
  the reference's card holds it in the footer (`js/ui.js:1064-1072`); the strip is lifted
  above the special so the reference's own reading order (desc, `statLine`, `⚡ special`)
  survives the port's shape. The team slot needs no such lift — the reference's scheda
  reads desc, special, `statLine` (`:968-971`), which is the order the port builds.

