```
$ gdlint godot/src/ui/data/UiData.gd godot/src/ui/screens/ChallengesScreen.gd \
         godot/src/ui/screens/HistoryScreen.gd godot/src/ui/components/ControlLegend.gd
Success: no problems found

$ gdlint godot/src/ui/focus/UiFocusBridge.gd
2 problems: the two pre-existing `max-returns` errors on `dispatch()` and `_act()`
(this wave removed three: the two class-definitions-order hits on the signal block and one
trailing space; the two return counts predate the wave and fixing them means restructuring
an audited function, which was not done)

$ gdlint godot/src/ui/screens/CharactersScreen.gd
15 problems, all pre-existing: file over `max-file-lines`, over `max-public-methods`,
11 long lines from earlier waves, 2 unused arguments. The three long lines this wave's
edits introduced were rewrapped before this run.

$ gdlint godot/tests/ui/theme_probe.gd
13 problems, the same 13 the file had before this wave (line-length/style in a tests
probe; no gdlint gate covers `godot/tests/`).

$ python3 uir_literal_scan.py     # the script below, verbatim
probe validation: 1 offender(s) -> ['Play now']      # the audit's own PROBE_SOURCE, as an
                                                     # instrument check of the transcription
scanned 29 of 30 UI scripts                          # UiStrings.gd exempt, as the audit exempts it
offenders: 0                                         # all five red lines gone
```

The transcription, so the check is reproducible without the engine:

```python
"""Transcription of the router scan's literal rule (router_audit.gd:_offenders_in_source),
run over godot/src/ui/**. Semantics kept: comments stripped at the first '#', any string
literal containing a space is an offender, developer-marker lines are skipped, the one
exempt file is skipped, the one whitelisted literal is tolerated. Validated by feeding it
the audit's own PROBE_SOURCE, which must yield exactly one offender: "Play now".
"""
import pathlib

MARKERS = ["push_error(", "push_warning(", "printerr(", "assert("]
ROOT = "godot/src/ui"
EXEMPT = {f"{ROOT}/UiStrings.gd"}
WHITELIST = {("godot/src/ui/PlaceholderScreen.gd", "not built yet")}


def literals(code):
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(code):
        if code[i] == '"':
            j, buf = i + 1, ""
            while j < len(code) and code[j] != '"':
                if code[j] == "\\" and j + 1 < len(code):
                    j += 1
                buf += code[j]
                j += 1
            out.append(buf)
            i = j
        i += 1
    return out


def offenders(path, source):
    out = []
    for number, line in enumerate(source.split("\n"), 1):
        code = line.split("#")[0]
        if any(marker in code for marker in MARKERS):
            continue
        for literal in literals(code):
            if " " in literal and (path, literal) not in WHITELIST:
                out.append((path, number, literal))
    return out


def scan():
    files = sorted(str(p) for p in pathlib.Path(ROOT).rglob("*.gd"))
    hits = [h for f in files if f not in EXEMPT for h in offenders(f, pathlib.Path(f).read_text())]
    return files, hits


if __name__ == "__main__":
    files, hits = scan()
    probe = ("probe", 'func x() -> void:\n\tlabel.text = "Play now"\n\tpush_error("dev only")\n')
    probe_hits = offenders(*probe)
    print(f"probe validation: {len(probe_hits)} offender(s) -> {[h[2] for h in probe_hits]}")
    print(f"scanned {len(files) - len(EXEMPT)} of {len(files)} UI scripts")
    print(f"offenders: {len(hits)}")
    for hit in hits:
        print(f"  {hit[0]}:{hit[1]} \"{hit[2]}\"")
```

(`src/ui` grows as the other workers land their components; the count moves, the rule
does not. At the time of this run the tree held 30 scripts, one of them exempt.)

The router scan's own transcription, the stat-strip derivation and the theme
transcription are the three checks that carry this wave; the audit-side checks (§7) are
the ones only the engine can run.

